insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('EC01', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Substitution effect', 'A decrease in the price of coffee, holding all else constant, causes a consumer to buy more coffee partly because coffee is now cheaper relative to tea. This portion of the increase in quantity demanded is best described as:', '["The substitution effect, as the consumer shifts spending toward the relatively cheaper good","The income effect, as the consumer''s real purchasing power rises","The wealth effect, as the consumer''s asset holdings become more valuable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The substitution effect captures the shift toward a good that has become relatively cheaper compared to substitutes, holding purchasing power constant. The income effect is the tempting confusion here: it describes the separate boost in real purchasing power from paying less for the same good, a distinct channel that also raises quantity demanded but for a different reason.'),
  ('EC02', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Movement vs shift', 'A rise in the price of gasoline, with all other factors held constant, causes consumers to purchase less gasoline. This change is best described as:', '["A leftward shift of the demand curve for gasoline","A movement along an unchanged demand curve for gasoline","A rightward shift of the supply curve for gasoline"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A change in a good''s own price, holding every other determinant of demand constant, moves the quantity demanded along a fixed demand curve rather than shifting the curve itself. Calling this a leftward shift of demand is the classic error of confusing a price-driven movement along the curve with a shift caused by a non-price determinant such as income or the price of a related good.'),
  ('EC03', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Price elasticity calc', 'The price of a good rises from $40 to $44, and quantity demanded falls from 500 units to 460 units. Using the percentage-change method, the price elasticity of demand (in absolute value) is closest to:', '["1.25","4.00","0.80"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Percentage change in quantity (8%) divided by percentage change in price (10%) gives an elasticity magnitude of 0.80, so demand is inelastic over this range. The 1.25 figure comes from inverting the ratio (price change over quantity change), and 4.00 results from dividing the raw 40-unit quantity drop by the 10% price change instead of first converting that quantity drop to a percentage.'),
  ('EC04', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Income elasticity calc', 'A household''s income rises from $60,000 to $66,000 (a 10% increase), and its annual restaurant spending quantity (meals purchased) rises from 40 meals to 46 meals (a 15% increase). The income elasticity of demand for restaurant meals is closest to, and this good is best classified as:', '["0.67, a necessity good","1.5, a luxury (superior) normal good","1.5, an inferior good"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Income elasticity equals the percentage change in quantity demanded (15%) divided by the percentage change in income (10%), or 1.5; because it is positive and greater than 1, restaurant meals behave as a luxury (superior) good here. The 0.67 figure inverts the ratio, and labeling a positive 1.5 elasticity as an inferior good confuses the sign rule -- inferior goods have negative income elasticity, not simply a large positive one.'),
  ('EC05', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Elasticity and revenue', 'A firm sells 100 units per week at a price of $20 (weekly total revenue of $2,000). It raises its price to $22, after which quantity demanded falls to 85 units per week. The firm''s new weekly total revenue, and what this implies about demand over this price range, is closest to:', '["$1,870, so demand is elastic over this range (total revenue fell as price rose)","$2,200, so demand is inelastic over this range (total revenue rose as price rose)","$1,700, so demand is elastic over this range (total revenue fell as price rose)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Total revenue at the new price is 22 x 85 = $1,870, below the original $2,000, so total revenue fell when price rose -- the definition of elastic demand. The $2,200 figure mistakenly pairs the new $22 price with the original 100-unit quantity, and $1,700 mistakenly pairs the original $20 price with the new 85-unit quantity -- both mixing values from different points on the demand curve instead of using the matched price-quantity pair at each point.'),
  ('EC06', 'Economics', 'Demand and Supply Analysis', 'Price ceiling shortage', 'A government imposes a price ceiling on rental apartments that is set below the market equilibrium rent. Holding other factors constant, the most likely result is:', '["A surplus of rental units, as landlords supply more housing at the lower price","No change in the quantity of housing supplied or demanded","A shortage of rental units, as quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the ceiling price"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A binding price ceiling set below equilibrium keeps price from rising to clear the market, so quantity demanded (which rises at the lower price) exceeds quantity supplied (which falls at the lower price), creating a shortage. The surplus outcome describes a price floor set above equilibrium instead, where the roles of supply and demand response are reversed.'),
  ('EC07', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Perfect competition traits', 'Which characteristic is most descriptive of a perfectly competitive market?', '["A small number of interdependent firms, each considering rivals'' likely reactions before changing price","Many firms selling a homogeneous product, each too small individually to affect the market price","A single seller controlling the entire market supply of a good with no close substitutes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Perfect competition features many small firms selling identical products, each acting as a price taker because no single firm''s output decision can move the market price. The interdependent-firms description fits oligopoly instead, and the single-seller description fits monopoly -- both real market structures, but not this one.'),
  ('EC08', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Monopoly MR vs MC', 'A profit-maximizing monopolist produces the output level at which marginal revenue equals marginal cost. At that quantity, price relative to marginal cost is best described as:', '["Price exceeds marginal cost, because the monopolist sets price from its downward-sloping demand curve above the marginal-revenue curve","Price equals marginal cost, exactly as in a perfectly competitive market","Price is below marginal cost, since the monopolist restricts output to raise price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the monopolist''s demand curve slopes downward, marginal revenue lies below price at every quantity, so setting MR = MC at the profit-maximizing quantity still leaves price above marginal cost. Assuming price equals marginal cost imports the perfect-competition result, where price and marginal revenue are the same curve -- a condition that no longer holds once a firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve.'),
  ('EC09', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Monopolist economic profit', 'A monopolist sells 2,000 units per month at a price of $18 per unit. Average total cost at that quantity is $13 per unit. The firm''s monthly economic profit is closest to:', '["$36,000","$26,000","$10,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Economic profit equals (price minus average total cost) times quantity: (18 - 13) x 2,000 = $10,000. The $36,000 figure is total revenue alone, price times quantity with cost never subtracted, and $26,000 is total cost alone, average total cost times quantity, with revenue never brought into the calculation.'),
  ('EC10', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Profit-max output calc', 'A firm faces the following at various output levels: at 4 units, marginal revenue is $50 and marginal cost is $50; at 5 units, marginal revenue is $42 and marginal cost is $58. At the profit-maximizing output of 4 units, price is $70 per unit and average total cost is $55 per unit. Total economic profit at the profit-maximizing output is closest to:', '["$280","$60","$15"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The firm should stop at 4 units, where MR equals MC exactly; producing a fifth unit would add $58 in cost for only $42 in extra revenue, reducing profit. At 4 units, economic profit is (70 - 55) x 4 = $60. The $280 figure is total revenue alone with cost never subtracted, and $15 is the correct $15 per-unit margin left un-multiplied by the 4 units actually sold.'),
  ('EC11', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Monopolistic competition traits', 'A market has many firms selling similar but not identical products (for example, differentiated by branding or features), with relatively low barriers to entry. This market structure is best described as:', '["Monopolistic competition","Oligopoly","Monopoly"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Many firms, product differentiation, and low barriers to entry are the defining features of monopolistic competition, distinct from the few interdependent firms of oligopoly or the single-seller structure of monopoly. Oligopoly is the tempting choice because it also allows differentiated products, but it is defined by a small number of firms, not many, and by strong strategic interdependence that monopolistic competition lacks.'),
  ('EC12', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Barriers to entry', 'Ranking the four market structures from lowest to highest barriers to entry, the correct order is:', '["Monopoly, oligopoly, monopolistic competition, perfect competition","Oligopoly, monopoly, perfect competition, monopolistic competition","Perfect competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, monopoly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Barriers to entry rise steadily from perfect competition (essentially none) through monopolistic competition (low) and oligopoly (high, often due to economies of scale or licensing) up to monopoly (highest, sometimes legally protected). The first choice reverses this entire order, and the second choice scrambles it out of sequence -- both fail to track barriers to entry monotonically across the four structures.'),
  ('EC13', 'Economics', 'Market Structures', 'Excess capacity', 'In the long-run equilibrium of a monopolistically competitive market, firms earn zero economic profit, but each firm still produces at a quantity below the level that would minimize average total cost. This outcome reflects:', '["A market failure caused by government price regulation","Productive inefficiency from excess capacity, a byproduct of product differentiation and a downward-sloping firm demand curve","A temporary condition that will not persist once new firms finish entering the market"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because each firm faces its own downward-sloping demand curve even after entry drives economic profit to zero, the tangency with average total cost occurs left of the minimum point, leaving firms with excess capacity -- a persistent, structural feature of the model, not a temporary one. Calling it temporary is the tempting error, since zero economic profit sounds like a completed adjustment, but the excess-capacity result is the long-run equilibrium itself, not a stage on the way to it.'),
  ('EC14', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'Expenditure approach', 'Using the expenditure approach, GDP is calculated as the sum of:', '["Consumption, gross private investment, government spending, and net exports","Wages, rents, interest, and profits earned by all factors of production","Consumption, gross private investment, government spending, and gross exports (imports excluded from the calculation entirely)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The expenditure approach sums consumption, gross private investment, government spending on goods and services, and net exports (exports minus imports). Summing factor incomes instead describes the income approach, a different but equally valid way to reach the same GDP total, and dropping imports from the net-exports term entirely, rather than subtracting them, would overstate GDP by ignoring spending on foreign-produced goods.'),
  ('EC15', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'Real vs nominal GDP', 'An analyst wants to compare a country''s economic output this year with its output ten years ago, isolating the change in actual production from the change in prices. The analyst should compare:', '["Nominal GDP in both years, since it reflects the current market value of output","Nominal GDP this year against real GDP ten years ago","Real GDP in both years, since it holds prices constant at base-year levels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Real GDP revalues each year''s output at constant base-year prices, so period-to-period changes reflect actual production, not price-level changes, which is exactly what a clean quantity comparison requires. Comparing nominal GDP across the two years would conflate real output growth with a decade of price inflation, overstating how much production actually grew.'),
  ('EC16', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'GDP deflator calc', 'An economy''s nominal GDP is $840 billion and its real GDP, measured in base-year prices, is $700 billion. The GDP deflator is closest to:', '["83.3","120","140"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The GDP deflator equals nominal GDP divided by real GDP, times 100: (840/700) x 100 = 120. The 83.3 figure inverts the ratio (real over nominal), and 140 simply subtracts real GDP from nominal GDP ($140 billion) and drops the units, mistaking a dollar difference for an index value.'),
  ('EC17', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'Real GDP calc', 'An economy''s nominal GDP for the year is $550 billion, and the GDP deflator is 110. Real GDP for the year is closest to:', '["$500 billion","$605 billion","$495 billion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Real GDP equals nominal GDP divided by the deflator expressed as a decimal: 550 / 1.10 = $500 billion. The $605 billion figure multiplies by 1.10 instead of dividing, and $495 billion comes from simply subtracting 10% of nominal GDP, treating the deflator as a flat percentage discount rather than the ratio it actually is.'),
  ('EC18', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'Nominal GDP growth', 'In a given year, an economy records consumption of $600 billion, gross private investment of $150 billion, government spending of $120 billion, exports of $90 billion, and imports of $110 billion. The following year, nominal GDP rises to $935 billion. The nominal GDP growth rate from the first year to the second year is closest to:', '["-2.6%","9.1%","10.0%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'First-year GDP is 600 + 150 + 120 + (90 - 110) = $850 billion, so growth to $935 billion is (935 - 850)/850 = 10.0%. The -2.6% figure forgets to subtract imports when totaling the expenditure components (600 + 150 + 120 + 90 = $960 billion instead of $850 billion), which flips the reported direction of GDP growth entirely, and 9.1% correctly finds the $85 billion change but divides by the second year''s $935 billion instead of the first year''s $850 billion base.'),
  ('EC19', 'Economics', 'Aggregate Output and Income', 'GDP vs GNP', 'A South Korean automaker operates a manufacturing plant in the United States, employing US workers and generating profit that is repatriated to South Korea. This plant''s output is counted in:', '["South Korea''s GDP, because the parent company is South Korean","US GDP, because GDP is based on where production physically occurs, regardless of the owner''s nationality","Neither country''s GDP, since GNP-based measures replaced GDP for cross-border production"]'::jsonb, 1, 'GDP is a domestic-production concept: it counts all output produced within a country''s borders, regardless of who owns the factors of production, so this plant''s output belongs in US GDP. Assigning it to South Korea''s GDP confuses GDP with GNP, which instead follows the nationality of ownership and would count the repatriated profit as South Korean income.'),
  ('EC20', 'Economics', 'Business Cycles', 'Business cycle phases', 'The phase of the business cycle immediately following the peak, characterized by a broad decline in economic activity, is called the:', '["Contraction (or recession)","Expansion","Trough"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The contraction phase follows the peak and describes the period of broadly declining output, income, and employment, ending at the trough. Expansion is the opposite phase, the period of rising activity that runs from the trough to the next peak, and the trough is only the single low turning point, not the declining phase itself.'),
  ('EC21', 'Economics', 'Business Cycles', 'Leading indicator', 'Which of the following is classified as a leading economic indicator, one that tends to change before the overall economy does?', '["The average duration of unemployment","Industrial production","Building permits for new private housing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Building permits reflect builders'' forward-looking commitments and typically turn before broader economic activity does, making them a leading indicator. Average duration of unemployment is a classic lagging indicator, since firms only lengthen or shorten unemployment spells well after a downturn or recovery is already underway, and industrial production moves roughly together with the broader economy, making it coincident rather than leading.'),
  ('EC22', 'Economics', 'Business Cycles', 'Lagging indicator', 'Which of the following is classified as a lagging economic indicator?', '["The average duration of unemployment","New orders for durable goods","Employees on nonfarm payrolls"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Average duration of unemployment tends to keep rising for a while even after a recovery begins, since employers wait to confirm a rebound before rehiring the long-term unemployed, making it a lagging indicator. New orders for durable goods is a leading indicator, since production plans respond to orders placed in advance, and nonfarm payroll employment is generally treated as a coincident indicator, moving alongside current economic activity.'),
  ('EC23', 'Economics', 'Business Cycles', 'Coincident indicator', 'Which of the following is classified as a coincident economic indicator, one that tends to move at roughly the same time as overall economic activity?', '["The stock market index","Industrial production","The average prime rate charged by banks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Industrial production tracks current output directly, so it rises and falls in step with the broader economy, making it a coincident indicator. Stock prices are forward-looking and classified as leading, since they respond to expected future earnings, and the average bank prime rate is a lagging indicator, since lenders typically adjust it only after economic conditions have already shifted.'),
  ('EC24', 'Economics', 'Business Cycles', 'Yield curve signal', 'An inverted yield curve, in which long-term government bond yields fall below short-term yields, is most notable in economic analysis because it has historically served as:', '["A coincident indicator, confirming a recession that is already underway","A lagging indicator, confirming a recovery only after it has taken hold","A leading indicator, often preceding a recession by several quarters"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An inverted yield curve has historically preceded recessions by several quarters, reflecting investors'' expectations of weaker future growth and lower future short-term rates, which is exactly what makes it a leading indicator. Treating it as coincident or lagging would mean waiting for a recession to already show up in current data before the yield curve reacts -- the opposite of its historical pattern of moving first.'),
  ('EC25', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'Structural unemployment', 'A skilled textile worker loses her job when a factory permanently closes due to automation, and must retrain for a different industry entirely. Her unemployment is best classified as:', '["Structural, arising from a mismatch between workers'' skills and available jobs","Frictional, arising from the normal time it takes to search for a new job","Cyclical, arising from a downturn in the overall business cycle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Structural unemployment arises when workers'' existing skills no longer match available jobs, often due to permanent shifts like automation, and typically requires retraining rather than a short search. Frictional unemployment is the tempting mislabel here, since it also involves time out of work, but frictional unemployment describes brief, normal job-to-job transitions, not a mismatch requiring retraining after a permanent job loss.'),
  ('EC26', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'Cost-push inflation', 'A sudden spike in global oil prices raises production costs across the economy, pushing the aggregate supply curve upward and increasing the overall price level. This is best described as:', '["Demand-pull inflation","Cost-push inflation","Structural inflation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cost-push inflation originates from rising input costs, such as oil, that shift aggregate supply and push up prices even without any change in aggregate demand. Demand-pull inflation instead originates from aggregate demand growing faster than aggregate supply, and structural inflation is not a standard classification alongside these two demand-side and supply-side categories.'),
  ('EC27', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'Unemployment rate calc', 'A country''s labor force totals 40 million people, of whom 36.4 million are employed. The unemployment rate is closest to:', '["9.9%","91.0%","9.0%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unemployment rate equals the unemployed (40 - 36.4 = 3.6 million) divided by the labor force of 40 million, or 9.0%. The 9.9% figure divides the unemployed by the number employed instead of the labor force, and 91.0% is the employment rate (employed over labor force), the complementary statistic rather than the one asked for.'),
  ('EC28', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'CPI inflation calc', 'The Consumer Price Index was 210.0 last year and is 216.3 this year. The annual inflation rate implied by these figures is closest to:', '["3.0%","2.9%","6.3%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inflation rate equals the change in CPI (216.3 - 210.0 = 6.3) divided by last year''s CPI of 210.0, or 3.0%. The 2.9% figure divides by this year''s CPI of 216.3 instead of last year''s, and 6.3% simply reports the raw point change in the index without dividing by the base CPI at all, confusing an index-point change with a percentage change.'),
  ('EC29', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'Labor force calc', 'A country''s working-age population is 250 million. The labor force participation rate is 64%, and 6% of the labor force is unemployed. The number of unemployed people is closest to:', '["15 million","9.6 million","90 million"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Labor force equals 250 million x 64% = 160 million, and unemployed equals 160 million x 6% = 9.6 million. The 15 million figure skips computing the labor force and applies the 6% unemployment rate directly to the entire working-age population instead, and 90 million confuses the 36% of the population that is not in the labor force at all with being unemployed -- but people outside the labor force (not working and not seeking work) are excluded from the unemployment count entirely, not counted as unemployed.'),
  ('EC30', 'Economics', 'Unemployment and Inflation', 'CPI substitution bias', 'The Consumer Price Index measures the cost of a fixed basket of goods and services over time. A widely cited criticism is that this fixed-basket approach tends to:', '["Understate true inflation, because it ignores new products entering the market","Perfectly measure the cost of living, since the basket is updated every month","Overstate true inflation, because consumers substitute toward relatively cheaper goods as prices change, a shift the fixed basket does not capture"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the CPI basket''s quantities are held fixed, it does not allow for consumers substituting away from goods that become relatively more expensive, so it overstates the true increase in the cost of living relative to what consumers actually experience -- the well-known substitution bias. The new-products criticism is a separate, real bias that also tends to overstate inflation, but it does not describe this fixed-basket substitution effect, and the basket''s composition is actually updated only periodically, not in real time every month.'),
  ('EC31', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Central bank tools', 'Which of the following is one of the traditional tools a central bank uses to implement monetary policy?', '["Adjusting the corporate income tax rate","Conducting open market operations, buying or selling government securities","Setting the minimum wage for private employers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Open market operations, the buying and selling of government securities to add or drain bank reserves, is one of the central bank''s core traditional tools, alongside the policy rate and reserve requirements. Adjusting tax rates and setting the minimum wage are both government tools, fiscal or regulatory, not instruments available to the central bank.'),
  ('EC32', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Policy rate cut', 'A central bank lowers its policy interest rate in response to weak economic growth. The most direct expected effect is:', '["Lower borrowing costs throughout the economy, encouraging spending and investment","An immediate increase in the reserve requirement for commercial banks","A reduction in the money supply, as banks hold more excess reserves"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lowering the policy rate reduces the cost of borrowing across the economy, which is the primary channel through which expansionary monetary policy stimulates spending and investment. A reserve requirement increase is a separate, and in fact opposite (contractionary), tool, and expansionary policy is associated with an increase, not a reduction, in the money supply as looser conditions encourage lending rather than reserve hoarding.'),
  ('EC33', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Open market operations', 'A central bank wants to increase the quantity of reserves in the banking system. Using open market operations, it should:', '["Raise the reserve requirement ratio for commercial banks","Sell government securities to commercial banks and the public","Purchase government securities from commercial banks and the public"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Purchasing government securities injects cash into the accounts of the sellers, which flows into the banking system as new reserves, directly increasing the quantity of reserves available. Selling securities does the opposite, draining reserves from the banking system, and raising the reserve requirement is a separate tool entirely that reduces the reserves banks have available to lend, rather than adding to the total supply of reserves.'),
  ('EC34', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Money multiplier calc', 'A banking system operates with a required reserve ratio of 8%, and banks hold no excess reserves. The money multiplier for this system is closest to:', '["8.0","12.5","0.08"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The simple money multiplier equals 1 divided by the required reserve ratio: 1 / 0.08 = 12.5. The 8.0 figure mistakes the reserve ratio''s percentage number itself for the multiplier, and 0.08 simply restates the reserve ratio as a decimal without ever taking its reciprocal.'),
  ('EC35', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Reserves to money supply', 'A central bank purchases $50 million in government securities from commercial banks. The required reserve ratio is 10%, banks hold no excess reserves, and there is no cash drain. The maximum resulting increase in the money supply is closest to:', '["$500 million","$50 million","$5 million"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The money multiplier is 1/0.10 = 10, so a $50 million injection of reserves can expand the money supply by up to 50 x 10 = $500 million as it recirculates through the banking system via repeated lending. The $50 million figure stops at the initial injection itself without applying the multiplier at all, and $5 million multiplies by the reserve ratio directly (50 x 0.10) instead of by its reciprocal, the actual multiplier.'),
  ('EC36', 'Economics', 'Monetary Policy', 'Reserve requirement hike', 'To combat rising inflation, a central bank raises the required reserve ratio for commercial banks. Holding all else equal, the most likely effect is:', '["An increase in the money multiplier, expanding the money supply","No effect on lending capacity, since reserve requirements apply only to central bank accounts","A decrease in banks'' excess reserves available to lend, contracting the money supply"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Raising the required reserve ratio forces banks to hold more of their deposits as reserves rather than lending them out, shrinking the money multiplier and contracting the money supply -- exactly the intended effect of tightening policy to fight inflation. The money multiplier moves in the opposite direction, since it equals 1 divided by the reserve ratio and therefore falls as the ratio rises, and reserve requirements apply directly to the deposits commercial banks hold, so they do constrain ordinary lending capacity rather than leaving it unaffected.'),
  ('EC37', 'Economics', 'Fiscal Policy', 'Fiscal policy tools', 'Fiscal policy, as distinct from monetary policy, is conducted primarily through:', '["Adjustments to the policy interest rate and bank reserve requirements","Government spending and taxation decisions","Changes in the growth rate of the money supply"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fiscal policy operates through the government''s own spending and tax decisions, made by the legislature or treasury rather than the central bank. Adjusting the policy rate, reserve requirements, and the money supply are all monetary policy tools, carried out by the central bank, a distinct institution with a distinct toolkit.'),
  ('EC38', 'Economics', 'Fiscal Policy', 'Automatic stabilizers', 'During an economic downturn, unemployment insurance payments to laid-off workers rise and total income tax collections fall, both without any new legislation being passed. This describes:', '["Automatic stabilizers, which cushion the business cycle through the existing structure of tax and transfer programs","Discretionary fiscal policy, since the government is actively responding to the downturn","Monetary policy operating through the banking system"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Automatic stabilizers like progressive income taxes and unemployment insurance adjust on their own as economic conditions change, cushioning aggregate demand during downturns without any new act of legislation. Discretionary fiscal policy is the tempting mislabel, but it specifically requires a new deliberate policy action, such as a newly passed stimulus bill, which is precisely what this scenario does not involve.'),
  ('EC39', 'Economics', 'Fiscal Policy', 'Spending multiplier calc', 'An economy''s marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is 0.75. The simple spending multiplier is closest to:', '["0.75","1.33","4.0"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The simple spending multiplier equals 1 divided by (1 minus the MPC): 1 / (1 - 0.75) = 1/0.25 = 4.0. The 0.75 figure simply restates the MPC without any further calculation, and 1.33 takes the reciprocal of the MPC directly (1/0.75) rather than first subtracting it from 1 to get the marginal propensity to save.'),
  ('EC40', 'Economics', 'Fiscal Policy', 'Multiplier and GDP', 'An economy''s marginal propensity to consume is 0.80. The government increases spending by $30 billion. Assuming no crowding out or other offsetting effects, the resulting change in equilibrium GDP is closest to:', '["$37.5 billion","$150 billion","$30 billion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spending multiplier is 1/(1 - 0.80) = 5, so a $30 billion increase in government spending raises equilibrium GDP by 30 x 5 = $150 billion as the initial spending recirculates through the economy. The $37.5 billion figure takes the reciprocal of the MPC directly (1/0.80 = 1.25) instead of first subtracting the MPC from 1, and $30 billion stops at the initial spending injection itself without applying any multiplier effect at all.'),
  ('EC41', 'Economics', 'Fiscal Policy', 'Crowding out', 'A government finances a large budget deficit by issuing additional bonds, which pushes up market interest rates and reduces private business investment. This effect is best described as:', '["Crowding out, where increased government borrowing displaces private investment","An automatic stabilizer, cushioning the economy during a downturn","Quantitative easing, expanding the central bank''s balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Crowding out describes exactly this channel: government borrowing to fund a deficit competes for loanable funds, raises interest rates, and displaces private investment that would otherwise have occurred. It is not an automatic stabilizer, which is a built-in tax-and-transfer response rather than a side effect of debt issuance, and quantitative easing is a central bank balance-sheet tool, unrelated to government deficit financing itself.'),
  ('EC42', 'Economics', 'International Trade', 'Comparative advantage basis', 'Country A can produce more of every good than Country B using the same resources. Despite this, mutually beneficial trade between the two countries can still occur because:', '["Comparative advantage requires one country to have an absolute advantage in at least one good","Comparative advantage depends on relative opportunity costs, not on which country produces more in absolute terms","Trade is only beneficial when both countries have roughly equal production capabilities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comparative advantage is about opportunity cost -- which country gives up less of one good to produce another -- so gains from trade exist even when one country is more productive at everything in absolute terms. The first choice gets the logic backwards: comparative advantage is precisely what still allows trade even without any absolute advantage on one side, and the third choice is simply untrue, since trade based on comparative advantage does not require the two countries to be similarly productive at all.'),
  ('EC43', 'Economics', 'International Trade', 'Opportunity cost calc', 'Using the same fixed set of resources, Country X can produce either 60 units of cloth or 40 units of wheat. Country Y can produce either 50 units of cloth or 100 units of wheat. Country X''s opportunity cost of producing one unit of wheat, in terms of cloth given up, is:', '["0.5 units of cloth","0.67 units of cloth","1.5 units of cloth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With the same resources, Country X can produce 60 cloth or 40 wheat, so producing 1 wheat costs 60/40 = 1.5 units of cloth forgone. The 0.5 figure comes from Country Y''s production numbers (50 cloth or 100 wheat, or 50/100), not Country X''s, and 0.67 answers the reversed question, the opportunity cost of 1 unit of cloth in terms of wheat (40/60), rather than the cost of wheat asked for here.'),
  ('EC44', 'Economics', 'International Trade', 'Comparative advantage direction', 'Country M can produce either 80 units of steel or 40 units of grain using its available resources. Country N can produce either 60 units of steel or 20 units of grain using the same amount of resources. Based on opportunity cost, Country M has a comparative advantage in:', '["Grain, since Country M gives up fewer units of steel to produce grain than Country N does","Steel, since Country M can produce more total steel than Country N","Both goods, since Country M produces more of each good in absolute terms"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Country M''s opportunity cost of grain is 80/40 = 2.0 units of steel, versus Country N''s 60/20 = 3.0 units of steel, so M gives up less steel to produce grain and holds the comparative advantage there, leaving N with the comparative advantage in steel. Producing more steel in absolute terms does not establish comparative advantage in steel, and no country can hold a comparative advantage in both goods simultaneously -- comparative advantage is inherently relative, so whichever good is not M''s comparative advantage must be N''s.'),
  ('EC45', 'Economics', 'International Trade', 'Terms of trade range', 'Country P can produce either 90 units of coffee or 30 units of sugar with its available resources; Country R can produce either 40 units of coffee or 80 units of sugar with the same amount of resources. Based on these opportunity costs, a mutually beneficial terms of trade for one unit of sugar, expressed in units of coffee, must fall:', '["Exactly 1.75 units of coffee per unit of sugar, the simple average of the two countries'' opportunity costs of sugar","Anywhere between 0.5 and 3.0 units of coffee per unit of sugar","Between 2.0 and 3.0 units of coffee per unit of sugar"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Country R''s opportunity cost of sugar is 40/80 = 0.5 units of coffee and Country P''s is 90/30 = 3.0 units of coffee, so any trade price between these two values leaves both countries better off than producing sugar domestically. The 1.75 figure averages the two opportunity costs into a single number, wrongly assuming there is one correct terms of trade rather than a full range of mutually beneficial prices, and the 2.0-to-3.0 range mismatches Country P''s opportunity cost of sugar with Country R''s opportunity cost of coffee (2.0) instead of R''s opportunity cost of sugar (0.5).'),
  ('EC46', 'Economics', 'International Trade', 'Tariff welfare loss', 'A country imposes a tariff on imported steel. Relative to free trade, this tariff is most likely to result in:', '["Lower prices for domestic steel consumers, as competition from imports intensifies","An increase in the quantity of steel imported, as foreign producers respond to higher demand","A net welfare loss to the domestic economy, even though domestic steel producers benefit and the government collects tariff revenue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A tariff raises the domestic price of steel, which benefits domestic producers and raises government revenue, but the resulting deadweight loss from reduced trade volume and higher prices paid by consumers exceeds these gains, producing a net loss to the domestic economy overall. Domestic consumers face higher, not lower, prices under a tariff, and the quantity imported falls rather than rises as the tariff makes foreign steel more expensive at the border.'),
  ('EC47', 'Economics', 'Foreign Exchange', 'Covered interest parity', 'The one-year interest rate on US dollar deposits is 5%, while the one-year interest rate on euro deposits is 2%. According to covered interest rate parity, the euro should trade in the one-year forward market, relative to its spot rate against the dollar, at a:', '["Forward premium, since the euro carries the lower interest rate","Forward discount, since the euro carries the lower interest rate","Forward rate identical to the spot rate, since interest rate differences are arbitraged away immediately in the spot market"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Covered interest rate parity requires the lower-interest-rate currency to trade at a forward premium relative to the higher-interest-rate currency, exactly offsetting the interest rate gap so that riskless arbitrage is not possible; here that is the euro. Expecting a forward discount for the euro reverses the parity relationship, and expecting the forward rate to simply equal the spot rate ignores that the whole point of covered interest rate parity is a systematic, predictable gap between them driven by the interest rate differential.'),
  ('EC48', 'Economics', 'Foreign Exchange', 'Cross rate calc', 'The USD/EUR exchange rate is 1.1000 (1 euro buys 1.1000 US dollars), and the USD/GBP exchange rate is 1.3200 (1 British pound buys 1.3200 US dollars). The EUR/GBP cross rate (the number of euros that 1 British pound buys) is closest to:', '["1.4520","1.2000","0.8333"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing the USD/GBP rate by the USD/EUR rate cancels the common USD unit and leaves EUR/GBP: 1.3200 / 1.1000 = 1.2000, meaning 1 pound buys 1.20 euros. The 1.4520 figure comes from multiplying the two rates instead of dividing them, which does not cancel a common unit at all, and 0.8333 inverts the correct division (1.10/1.32), giving GBP/EUR instead of the EUR/GBP rate asked for.'),
  ('EC49', 'Economics', 'Foreign Exchange', 'Forward premium calc', 'The spot USD/EUR exchange rate is 1.2000, and the 180-day forward USD/EUR rate is 1.2180. The euro''s forward premium or discount, expressed as an annualized percentage, is closest to:', '["1.5% discount","1.5% premium","3.0% premium"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 180-day forward rate exceeds spot by (1.2180 - 1.2000)/1.2000 = 1.5% over that six-month period; annualizing by multiplying by 360/180 = 2 gives a 3.0% annualized forward premium for the euro. The 1.5% premium figure correctly identifies the direction but stops before annualizing the six-month change, and 1.5% discount is doubly wrong, understating the annualized magnitude and mislabeling the euro as weaker forward when the higher forward rate actually means it is stronger.'),
  ('EC50', 'Economics', 'Foreign Exchange', 'PPP forward rate', 'The current spot exchange rate is USD/GBP 1.3000 (1 British pound buys 1.3000 US dollars). Expected annual inflation is 5% in the United States and 2% in the United Kingdom. According to relative purchasing power parity, the expected spot exchange rate in one year is closest to:', '["$1.263","$1.300","$1.338"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Relative PPP projects the future spot rate as the current spot rate times (1 + US inflation) / (1 + UK inflation): 1.3000 x (1.05/1.02) = 1.3382, since the higher-inflation US dollar is expected to depreciate, meaning more dollars are needed to buy one pound. The $1.263 figure flips the ratio (1.02/1.05), incorrectly treating the lower-inflation currency as the one that depreciates, and $1.300 assumes the exchange rate stays at its current spot level, ignoring the inflation differential entirely.');
